eling such fine-grained syntactic structures compared to the later layers. 4.4 Omni-NegCLIP Fine-tuning Pipeline To enable the final Omni-NegCLIP model to simultaneously understand both types of negation tasks, we optimize the model using a combined objective in each fine-tuning step, where the total loss is defined as the sum of ℒ p \mathcal{L}_{p} and ℒ a \mathcal{L}_{a} . Furthermore, as revealed in Section 4.3 , the front transformer layers exhibit stronger negation learning capability than the later layers. Therefore, we fine-tune only the front transformer layers ℱ = { ℓ k } k = 1 K \mathcal{F}=\{\ell_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K} and the projection layer 𝒫 ⁡ ( ⋅ ) \mathcal{P}(\cdot) , while keeping all other parameters frozen. The detailed procedure of one fine-tuning step is described in Algorithm 1 . Algorithm 1 Omni-NegCLIP Fine-tuning Input: Pretrained text encoder ℰ t ​ e ​ x ​ t ​ ( ⋅ ) \mathcal{E}_{text}(\cdot) ; Front transformer layers ℱ = { ℓ k } k = 1 K \mathcal{F}=\{\ell_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K} ; Projection layer 𝒫 ⁡ ( ⋅ ) \mathcal{P}(\cdot) ; Presence-based batch ℬ p = { ( I p ( i ) , C p ( i ) , C p ′ ( i ) ) } i = 1 B \mathcal{B}_{p}=\{(I_{p}^{(i)},C_{p}^{(i)},C_{p}^{\prime(i)})\}_{i=1}^{B} ; Absence-based batch ℬ a = { ( I a ( i ) , C a ( i ) , C a ′ ( i ) ) } i = 1 B \mathcal{B}_{a}=\{(I_{a}^{(i)},C_{a}^{(i)},C_{a}^{\prime(i)})\}_{i=1}^{B} Output: Updated ℰ t ​ e ​ x ​ t ​ ( ⋅ ) \mathcal{E}_{text}(\cdot) 1 /* Compute losses in one fine-tuning step */ 2 ℒ p ← f p ​ ( ℬ p ) \mathcal{L}_{p}\leftarrow f_{p}(\mathcal{B}_{p}) ; 3 ℒ a ← f a ​ ( ℬ a ) \mathcal{L}_{a}\leftarrow f_{a}(\mathcal{B}_{a}) ; 4 ℒ t ​ o ​ t ​ a ​ l ← ℒ p + ℒ a \mathcal{L}_{total}\leftarrow\mathcal{L}_{p}+\mathcal{L}_{a} ; 5 /* Update parameters */ 6 ℰ t ​ e ​ x ​ t ​ ( ⋅ ) ← update ​ ( ℰ t ​ e ​ x ​ t ​ ( ⋅ ) , ℒ t ​ o ​ t ​ a ​ l , ℱ , 𝒫 ⁡ ( ⋅ ) ) \mathcal{E}_{text}(\cdot)\leftarrow	ext{update}\!\left(\mathcal{E}_{text}(\cdot),\mathcal{L}_{total},\mathcal{F},\mathcal{P}(\cdot)ight) ; 7 return ℰ 